      <h2>The Path to a One-Arm Hang</h2>
      <p>A full one-arm dead hang puts about twice the load on each arm that a two-arm hang does. Jumping straight there risks finger pulley and shoulder irritation. The safer route is a gradual reduction of assistance over about 6 to 9 weeks after you reach the two-arm base.</p>
      <p>Each phase removes one finger of support from your assist hand. Each finger you remove shifts more load to your working arm. By the final assisted phase your working arm already carries nearly all of your bodyweight. Removing the last finger is a small step.</p>
      <p>Heavier athletes usually need longer at each phase. Not everyone reaches a full one-arm hang. Follow the phases in order. The <a href="/one-arm-deadhang/">one-arm dead hang overview</a> covers benefits and the prerequisite in more detail. The doses on this page are common training practice, not a tested prescription.</p>

      <h2>Phase 1: Two-Arm Mastery</h2>
      <p>Build a controlled two-arm hang of about 60 seconds and about 30 seconds of active hang before starting this progression. This is a conservative guide, not a tested threshold. Skipping the two-arm base raises the chance of finger and elbow irritation.</p>
      <p>Train on non-consecutive days with 3 or 4 sets of controlled holds. Stop a second or two before your grip gives out. Use an overhand grip at shoulder width and record every set in a training log. Hold about 60 seconds across 3 consecutive sessions before moving to Phase 2.</p>
      <p>Add <a href="/passive-vs-active-hang/">active hang sets</a> to build the scapular stability you need for one-arm work. Your shoulder stabilizers must handle about twice the load per side. Weak stabilizers often limit one-arm attempts before the grip does.</p>

      <h2>Phase 2: Four-Finger Assist</h2>
      <p>Grip the bar fully with your dominant hand. Place only four fingers of your off-hand on the bar for minimal assistance. Keep the assist hand grip light. Your working arm carries most of your bodyweight.</p>
      <p>Target 15-20 seconds per set. Perform 3 sets per arm. Always train both arms even if you only plan to test one arm eventually. Rest 90 seconds between sets.</p>
      <p>Spend 1-2 weeks at this phase. Progress to Phase 3 when you hold 20 seconds for 3 consecutive sets on both arms. The assist hand should feel like a balance aid rather than a strength supplement.</p>

      <h2>Phase 3: Reducing Fingers</h2>
      <p>Remove one finger from your assist hand at each step. Go from three fingers to two and then to one. Each reduction shifts more load to your working arm.</p>

      <h3>Three-Finger Assist (Week 3-4)</h3>
      <p>Three fingers provide noticeable support. Target 12-15 seconds per set for 3 sets per arm. Progress when you hit 15 seconds consistently.</p>

      <h3>Two-Finger Assist (Week 4-5)</h3>
      <p>Two fingers offer minimal support. Target 10-12 seconds per set. This phase reveals whether your grip and shoulder are ready for full one-arm work. If hold times drop below 8 seconds spend an extra week here.</p>

      <h3>One-Finger Assist (Week 5-6)</h3>
      <p>A single finger acts as a safety net rather than real assistance. Target 8 to 10 seconds per set. Many people need only about 1 week at this phase because the gap between one-finger assist and no assist is small.</p>

      <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Scrollable comparison table" tabindex="0"><table class="data-table">
        <thead>
