      <h2>Phase 4: Full One-Arm Hang</h2>
      <p>Remove your assist hand completely. Grip the bar with your working hand centered directly above your shoulder. Step off the platform with control. Hold for 5 seconds on your first clean attempt.</p>
      <p>Build from 5 seconds toward 10 to 15 seconds over 2 to 3 weeks, adding 1 to 2 seconds per session. Do not chase big jumps. Tendons adapt more slowly than muscle.</p>
      <p>Expect slight body rotation toward the hanging arm. This is normal. Allow the rotation rather than fighting it.</p>
      <p>Breathe steadily through your nose. Holding your breath tends to shorten a hold.</p>

      <h2>One-Arm Hang Safety</h2>
      <p>Stop at sharp shoulder, elbow or finger pain. Heavier people should progress more slowly. A one-arm hang should never be attempted over a hard floor.</p>
      <p>The <a href="/deadhang-safety/">dead hang safety guide</a> covers bar checks, stepping down and when to see a clinician.</p>

      <h2>Weekly Training Schedule</h2>
      <p>Train 3 times a week on non-consecutive days with at least one rest day between hard sessions. Leaving rest days lowers the chance of tendon irritation in the forearm flexors.</p>

      <div class="callout">
        <h3>3-Day Schedule</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Day 1 (Monday):</strong> 3 sets per arm at current phase. Focus on hold time.</li>
          <li><strong>Day 2 (Wednesday):</strong> 4 sets per arm at current phase. Focus on volume.</li>
          <li><strong>Day 3 (Friday):</strong> 3 sets per arm. Test at next phase for 1-2 sets. Record all times.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <p>Record hold times for every set on both arms. Track the working arm and the assist configuration (how many fingers). Progress to the next phase when you hit your target time for 3 consecutive sessions on the Friday test sets.</p>
      <p>Always train your weaker arm first while your grip is freshest, and give it equal volume.</p>

      <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
      <h3>One Arm Is Much Weaker</h3>
      <p>Most people grip a little harder with one hand; about 10% is the figure usually quoted for right-handers, with little difference in left-handers. Train the weak arm first in every session. Add one extra set on the weak side and stay at the current phase until it catches up. Do not advance the strong arm more than one phase ahead.</p>

      <h3>Shoulder Rotation Feels Excessive</h3>
      <p>Some body rotation is normal in a one-arm hang. Add side planks and two-arm active hangs to your warm-up if you rotate a lot. They build the trunk and shoulder control that steadies the hold.</p>

      <h3>Grip Gives Out Before Shoulder</h3>
      <p>Use chalk if sweat, not fatigue, is ending your hang. If chalk does not solve the problem, your fingers need more volume at the current phase. Add one extra set per session and extend the phase by one week.</p>

      <h3>Elbow Pain During One-Arm Work</h3>
      <p>Pain on the inside of the elbow can mean the flexor tendons are overloaded. Drop back a phase and rest. See a clinician if the pain persists. Read the <a href="/injury-prevention/">injury prevention guide</a> before you resume.</p>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How long should I be able to two-arm dead hang before attempting a one-arm hang?</h3>
          <p>Before one-arm progressions, start with a controlled two-arm hang of about 60 seconds and about 30 seconds of active hang. These are conservative guides, not tested thresholds. They give your joints and tendons a base before each arm carries about twice the load it carries in a two-arm hang.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How long does it take to achieve a full one-arm dead hang?</h3>
          <p>The phases on this page add up to about 6 to 9 weeks after the two-arm base. Heavier athletes usually need longer at each phase and some people do not reach a full one-arm hang. The method is progressive finger removal from the assist hand rather than jumping straight to unsupported hanging.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Is it normal for my body to rotate during a one-arm hang?</h3>
          <p>Some body rotation toward the hanging arm is normal in a one-arm hang. Add two-arm active hangs and side planks to your warm-up if you rotate a lot. Stay at the current phase until the rotation settles.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>What should I do if one arm is much weaker than the other?</h3>
          <p>Train your weaker arm first in every session while your grip is freshest. Add one extra set on the weak side and stay at the current phase until it catches up. Do not advance the strong arm more than one phase ahead of the weak arm.</p>
        </div>

